  "full_response": "The **Actor Model** is a conceptual framework for concurrent and distributed computing, first proposed by Carl Hewitt in 1973 and later developed by Gul Agha and others. It provides a high-level abstraction for writing concurrent programs by treating \"actors\" as the fundamental units of computation.\n\n### **Core Principles of the Actor Model**\n1. **Actors as Universal Primitives**\n   - Everything in the system is an **actor**—a lightweight, independent computational entity.\n   - Actors encapsulate their own state and behavior.\n   - They do not share memory (no shared state), avoiding race conditions.\n\n2. **Message Passing (Asynchronous Communication)**\n   - Actors communicate **only** via **asynchronous messages** (no direct method calls).\n   - Messages are sent to an actor’s **mailbox** (a message queue).\n   - Actors process messages **one at a time** (sequential processing within an actor).\n\n3. **No Shared State**\n   - Actors **do not share mutable state**; instead, they exchange immutable messages.\n   - This eliminates the need for locks, semaphores, or other synchronization mechanisms.\n\n4. **Behavior Changes via Messages**\n   - An actor’s behavior can change in response to messages (e.g., finite-state machines).\n   - Example: A `BankAccount` actor might reject withdrawals if the balance is insufficient.\n\n5. **Location Transparency**\n   - Actors can be local or distributed across machines without changing the programming model.\n   - The system handles message routing transparently.\n\n6. **Dynamic Creation & Supervision**\n   - Actors can **spawn** new actors dynamically.\n   - Some implementations (e.g., Erlang/OTP) support **supervision trees** for fault tolerance.\n\n---\n\n### **How the Actor Model Works**\n1. **An actor has:**\n   - A **mailbox** (FIFO queue for incoming messages).\n   - A **behavior** (how it processes messages).\n   - A **state** (private data, modified only by the actor itself).\n\n2. **When a message arrives:**\n   - The actor processes it **sequentially** (no concurrency within an actor).\n   - It may:\n     - Send messages to other actors.\n     - Create new actors.\n     - Change its own behavior (e.g., switch to a different state).\n\n3. **Example (Pseudocode):**\n   ```erlang\n   % A simple counter actor in Erlang\n   loop(Count) ->\n",
